"""Replace the provisional inventory with precise statements and actual checks."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv

root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
p=root/'STATEMENTS.tsv'
with p.open() as stream:
    reader=csv.DictReader(stream,delimiter='\t');fields=reader.fieldnames;rows=list(reader)
precise={
1:r'''Programme on the cover: construct modules from functors and prorepresent a functor along a section. Our precise affine realization: for an augmented commutative A-algebra C→A with ideal I, its formal functor on augmented A-algebras with nilpotent augmentation ideal is prorepresented by {C/I^(n+1)}. On square-zero extensions A⊕M its tangent functor is Hom_A(I/I²,M), with I/I²=Ω¹_(C/A)⊗_C A and a surjection Sym_A(I/I²)→gr_I(C). No universal prorepresentability claim for arbitrary functors follows from the title.''',
2:r'''For a ring map A→B, restriction of scalars sends Hom_A(P,−) to Hom_B(B⊗_A P,−). A finite-limit-preserving functor F:Mod(A)→Sets has a canonical additive A-linear module structure. If it also preserves filtered colimits, F(M)⊗_A B→F(M⊗_A B) is an isomorphism for every flat A-algebra B and every A-module M; in particular this holds for localization.''',
3:r'''For a faithfully flat ring map A→B, a functor F:Mod(A)→Sets is left exact and represented by an A-module if and only if it is left exact and its restriction to Mod(B) is represented by a B-module. Finite generation and finite presentation of the representing module descend and ascend separately. No noetherian hypothesis is needed.''',
4:r'''Let A be noetherian and f∈A. A functor F:Mod(A)→Sets is represented by a finite A-module if and only if it preserves finite limits and filtered colimits, its restrictions to A_f and to every A/(f^n), n≥1, are representable, and for every finite module M over C=lim_n A/(f^n), F(M)→lim_n F(M/f^nM) is bijective. The formal test modules need not be completions of finite A-modules.''',
5:r'''Let A be noetherian. Finite representability of F is equivalent to left exactness, preservation of filtered colimits, the principal-completion comparison of H035.4 for every f∈A, and this generic condition: for every nonzero quotient B of A there is a nonnilpotent g∈B such that F restricted to Mod(B_g) is representable. Literally including the zero quotient makes the necessity direction false.''',
6:r'''Let (A,m) be noetherian local. A left exact functor F preserving filtered colimits is represented by a finite A-module if F(M)→lim_n F(M/m^nM) is bijective for every finite Ahat-module M and F(E_n) is finite for the residue-field injective hull E_n over each A/m^n. The page's sufficient assumption that F takes every finite original A-module to a finite module implies these tests. The displayed stronger finite-value condition over Ahat is also sufficient. Necessity holds for finite representable F.''',
7:r'''Let A be noetherian and F:Mod(A)→Sets preserve finite limits and filtered colimits. Suppose for every p∈Spec(A) it commutes with maximal-ideal reduction on every finite A_p-hat module, and takes every finite-length A_p-module to a finite A_p-module. Then F restricted to Mod(A_p) is represented by a finite A_p-module Q_p, with Q_p⊗_(A_p) A_q=Q_q for every q⊂p.''',
8:r'''Under all hypotheses of H035.7, assume additionally that for every prime p there is a nonzero f∈A/p for which F on Mod((A/p)_f) is representable. Then F is a filtered colimit of Hom_A(P_i,−), with finite P_i and surjective inverse transition maps; after localization at any fixed prime the system is eventually stationary. For every finite M and α∈F(M) there is a unique smallest submodule defining α. The written condition allowing f=0 is insufficient.''',
9:r'''Let A be noetherian and F be an additive A-linear functor preserving filtered colimits. If on each integral quotient B of A its restriction is represented by a finite module on some nonempty open, then p↦dim_(k(p))F(k(p)) is a constructible integer-valued function on Spec(A). Generic normality is unnecessary for this direction.''',
10:r'''Under H035.7, constructibility of p↦dim F(k(p)), even together with a dense normal open in every integral quotient, does not imply generic representability. Counterexample: A=Z, L=Σ_(p prime)(1/p)Z⊂Q, F=L⊗−. The corrected implication holds if F(B) is finite for every integral quotient B: then on a constant-rank open the local representing modules are free and F is represented by the dual of the finite locally free module F(B).''',
11:r'''For a locally noetherian scheme S, its nilradical J, and a coherent module F, there is a dense open U such that every graded piece of gr_J(O_S) and gr_J(F), restricted to U, is locally free over O_(U_red). This is a finite graded-freeness assertion locally; it does not assert that every noetherian scheme has a dense Cohen–Macaulay open.''',
12:r'''For a locally noetherian scheme S and coherent F, there is a dense open U such that for every s∈U, every O_(S,s)-regular sequence x_1,…,x_r, and every i>0, Tor_i^(O_(S,s))(F_s,O_(S,s)/(x_1,…,x_r))=0. The module denoted M without definition in the written proof is reconstructed as the coefficient module; the filtration proof uses all positive Tor degrees.''',
13:r'''For coherent modules P,Q on a locally noetherian scheme S, there is a dense open U such that for all s∈U and every regular sequence x in O_(S,s), Hom(P,Q)_s⊗O_(S,s)/(x)→Hom_(O_(S,s))(P_s,Q_s/(x)Q_s) is an isomorphism. The same parameter list is used on both sides in our reconstruction of the doubtful final index.''',
14:r'''For a finite presentation L_1→L_0→P→0 with finite free L_i and coefficient Q, Hom(P,Q) is H⁰ of [L_0^∨⊗Q→L_1^∨⊗Q]. The manuscript's two occurrences of P instead of Q give a false formula, already for P=k and Q=k² over a field. The corrected formula also computes Hom(P,Q⊗M) after tensoring this complex with M.''',
15:r'''For a two-term cochain complex K=[K⁰→K¹] and module M, the written conditions Tor_1(M,H¹(K))=Tor_1(M,Z¹(K))=0 do not ensure H⁰(K)⊗M→H⁰(K⊗M) is an isomorphism. Counterexample: A=k[x,y]/(xy), M=A/(x), K=[A --y--> A]. A sufficient corrected condition is Tor_1(M,H¹(K))=Tor_1(M,B¹(K))=0, where B¹=im(d); alternatively retain Tor_1(M,K¹)=0 and require Tor_1(M,H¹(K))=Tor_2(M,H¹(K))=0.''',
16:r'''Our canonical-module interpretation of the heading: for a noetherian Cohen–Macaulay local ring A and finite module Ω, Ω is canonical if and only if, for every system of parameters x, Ω/(x)Ω is the injective hull of the residue field as an A/(x)-module. Canonical means that its completion is the normalized canonical module of Ahat. The literal term differentials is retained; interpreting it as Kähler differentials would be false, for example Ω¹_(k/k)=0 rather than k.''',
17:r'''Let X be a locally noetherian Cohen–Macaulay scheme with a canonical (fundamental, in the reconstructed terminology) sheaf Ω. For an O_X-regular sequence of sections x_1,…,x_r defining Y, the coherent sheaf Ω/(x)Ω is a canonical sheaf on the Cohen–Macaulay scheme Y. The assertion is local on X and includes the empty sequence.''',
18:r'''Under H035.17, for x∈X and every system of parameters t of the Cohen–Macaulay local ring O_(X,x), Ω_x/(t)Ω_x is the indecomposable injective hull of k(x) over the artinian local ring O_(X,x)/(t). It is also an injective cogenerator of the finite-module category there.''',
19:r'''If X is locally noetherian and locally embeddable in a regular scheme, there are a dense open U⊂X and a coherent module Ω on X such that U is Cohen–Macaulay and Ω_x is canonical for all x∈U. Thus every parameter quotient gives H035.18 and its Hom detection property. Our stronger sufficient hypothesis is Gor-2: locally, every integral closed subscheme has a nonempty Gorenstein open.''',
20:r'''The conclusion of H035.19 also holds for a locally noetherian excellent scheme, because every integral quotient has a dense regular, hence Gorenstein, open. This is generic canonical-module existence; it does not assert existence of a global dualizing complex on every excellent scheme.''',
21:r'''The conclusion of H035.19 does not follow merely from the EGA IV 6.11.8 condition CMU, which asks for a nonempty Cohen–Macaulay open in every integral closed subscheme. A noetherian one-dimensional domain obtained by localizing k[t_i³,t_i⁴,t_i⁵:i≥1] away from its block maximal ideals is Cohen–Macaulay but has non-Gorenstein points in every nonempty open, so admits no finite generic canonical module. Gor-2 is the corrected uniform all-integral-quotients hypothesis; its sufficiency is H035.19 and its necessity for that uniform assertion follows from generic freeness of a rank-one canonical module.''',
22:r'''On the inserted absolute-value typescript, multiplicativity, subadditivity, and f(x)=0⇒x=0 alone do not make f(x−y) a distance: f≡1 is a counterexample. Add f(0)=0. Then the resulting normalized absolute value gives a field topology. A real additive valuation v, with v(0)=+∞, gives the absolute value exp(−v), satisfying the maximum inequality. This arithmetic leaf is outside H035 and its authorship is not established.''',
23:r'''For a normalized absolute value f on a field K, the following are equivalent: f=exp(−v) for a real additive valuation; f(x+y)≤max(f(x),f(y)); f(n·1)≤1 for all natural n. Equivalently the values on the prime subring are bounded. The binomial estimate f(x+y)^n≤(n+1)max(f(x),f(y))^n proves the nontrivial implication.''',
24:r'''Every normalized absolute value on a field of characteristic p>0 is valuative. The supporting sentence that every n·1 is a root of unity is false at n=p. The corrected supporting statement is that every nonzero element of the prime field has finite multiplicative order; zero has absolute value zero.''',
25:r'''For normalized absolute values f,g on a field, equivalence is the relation f=g^s for some strictly positive real s, and is equivalent to inducing the same field topology. The unqualified real exponent in the typescript permits s=0 on K^×, destroying symmetry, and leaves 0^0 undefined at zero. Both trivial and nontrivial topology cases are included in the corrected proof.''',
26:r'''A normalized absolute value on Q is either trivial, a positive power |·|_p^s of the p-adic absolute value for a unique prime p and s>0, or a power of the ordinary absolute value with 0<s≤1. Thus the classification by the two nontrivial types must either assume nontriviality or include the trivial case separately. The completed archimedean proof is ours, since this leaf stops mid-argument.''',
27:r'''Let A be noetherian Cohen–Macaulay local, Ω a finite module with the parameter-injective property in H035.18, and u:R→R′ a map of finite modules. If Hom(R′,Ω/qΩ)→Hom(R,Ω/qΩ) is an isomorphism for every parameter ideal q, then u is an isomorphism. The page assumes u surjective; our proof shows this assumption is unnecessary. A single parameter ideal does not suffice.''',
28:r'''In the cancelled draft, let A=O_(X,x) for x∈U of Corollaire 10, Ω=Ω_x, and u:R→R′ a surjection of finite modules. Use the final rewritten functors Φ_R(M)=Hom(R,M), Φ_(R′)(M)=Hom(R′,M). If both commute on Ω with every parameter quotient and Hom(u,Ω) is an isomorphism, then u is an isomorphism. The page's regular-sequence comparison is stronger and implies the needed parameter comparisons because A is Cohen–Macaulay.''',
29:r'''On an affine U with the finite canonical module Ω of Corollaire 10, suppose F=colim_i Hom(P_i,−), F(Ω) is finite, and its canonical comparisons with Ω_p/qΩ_p are isomorphisms for all p and all parameter ideals q. Eventual isomorphism of the transition maps requires finite P_i and a strict inverse system. Without strictness the zero-transition system P_i=k over a field satisfies the tests but is never stationary. With finite terms and surjective transitions, the system is eventually stationary and F is represented by its eventual term.''',
30:r'''Let A be a noetherian quotient of a regular noetherian ring and F:Mod(A)→Sets. Then F=Hom_A(P,−) for a finite P if and only if: (i) F preserves filtered colimits; (ii) F preserves finite limits; (iii a) for every prime p and every finite A_p-hat module M, F(M)→lim_n F(M/(pA_p)^nM) is bijective; (iii b) for every a∈A and every finite module M over the (a)-adic completion of A, F(M)→lim_n F(M/a^nM) is bijective; (iv) F(M) is finite for every finite A-module M; (v) p↦dim_(k(p))F(k(p)) is constructible; (vi) for every nonzero quotient B of A and every finite B-module Ω, some nonnilpotent b∈B makes F(Ω)⊗_B B_p/q→F(Ω⊗_B B_p/q) bijective for every p∈D(b) and every parameter ideal q of B_p. The canonical module structures follow from (ii).''',
31:r'''In H035.30 one may instead assume A noetherian with the property that every integral quotient B has a nonempty regular principal open. Under (i), (ii), (iv), (vi), condition (v) then follows; the full representability criterion remains valid with the two completion conditions and without a separate rank condition. The regular-quotient-of-a-ring hypothesis is not retained.''',
32:r'''Let f:X→S be proper and finitely presented. Let F,G be finitely presented quasi-coherent O_X-modules and suppose G is flat over S. There is a finitely presented quasi-coherent P on S, with a natural isomorphism Hom_sheaf(P,M)=f_*Hom_sheaf(F,G⊗f^*M) for every quasi-coherent M on S. The representing object and isomorphism commute with arbitrary base change. Uniqueness is uniqueness respecting the representation.''',
33:r'''Under H035.32, allow F to be arbitrary quasi-coherent, while G remains finitely presented and S-flat. The same representation and arbitrary-base-change result hold with P quasi-coherent, without a finiteness assertion. If locally F=colim_i F_i with finitely presented terms, then P=colim_i P_i in the same index direction.''',
34:r'''Let A be a noetherian quotient of a regular noetherian ring, X proper over Spec(A), and F,G coherent on X. For each i,j≥0, the additive A-linear functors M↦H^j(X,Ext_sheaf^i(F,G⊗_A M)) and M↦Ext_X^j(F,G⊗_A M) satisfy H035.30(i),(iii a,b),(iv),(v),(vi). Tensor is underived and G need not be A-flat. Left exactness is not asserted and can fail. The claim does not identify this whole class with Auslander coherent functors.''',
35:r'''Let X be of finite type over a noetherian ring A, F,G coherent on X, Ω a finite A-module, and N≥0. There is a dense open U⊂Spec(A) such that, for every p∈U, every A_p-regular sequence x, and 0≤i≤N, Ext_sheaf_X^i(F,G⊗_A Ω)⊗_A A_p/(x)→Ext_sheaf_X^i(F,G⊗_A Ω⊗_A A_p/(x)) is an isomorphism. Ext is computed using the original source F, so no unproved exactness of its base-changed resolution is assumed.''',
36:r'''Let K be a bounded complex of A-modules and M an A-module. If Tor_i^A(M,K^j)=Tor_i^A(M,H^j(K))=0 for all i>0 and all j, then the canonical map H^j(K)⊗_A M→H^j(K⊗_A M) is an isomorphism for every j. There is no finiteness or flatness hypothesis beyond these Tor conditions.''',
37:r'''Let X be of finite type over a noetherian ring A and R coherent on X. There is a dense open U⊂Spec(A) such that for every p∈U, every A_p-regular sequence x, and all i>0, the sheaf Tor_i^A(R,A_p/(x)) vanishes. The proof requires generic flatness of the finitely many graded coefficient pieces as well as the graded pieces of A. The inserted R/displayed F mismatch is definite; the first graded coefficient in the ink remains uncertain.''',
38:r'''For a proper morphism X→Spec(A), with A noetherian and E coherent on X, there is a dense open U such that H^i(X,E)⊗_A A_p/(x)→H^i(X,E⊗_A A_p/(x)) is an isomorphism for all p∈U, all A_p-regular sequences x, and all i. The page's literal p∈S instead of p∈U is false: on P¹_(k[t]), the extension of O by O(−2) with class t has H⁰(E)=0 but H⁰(E_0)=k.''',
39:r'''Prospective constructible-functor programme. Our exact affine question takes additive A-linear functors Mod(A)→Mod(A), with A a noetherian quotient of a regular noetherian ring, satisfying H035.30(i),(iii a,b),(iv),(v),(vi), and asks whether their composition satisfies the same axioms. Filtered colimits, finite values, constructible ranks, and (vi) are proved stable. Only completion remains open. Full closure is proved for Dedekind domains and artinian rings; maximal-ideal completion is proved stable for countable A. Over a countable local A, finiteness over principal completions is also proved, leaving the inverse-system continuity problem.''',
40:r'''The proposed two-scheme class Qcoh(Y)→Qcoh(X) is not defined on the page. The contextual affine realizations Ext_A^i(P,−), Tor_i^A(Q,−), for finite P,Q over a noetherian quotient A of a regular ring, and M↦H^q(X,E⊗_A M) for proper X/A and coherent E, satisfy the non-left-exact axioms of H035.30. An unqualified assertion for arbitrary direct image is false: A¹_k→Spec(k) sends O to k[t], violating finite values. The general two-scheme formulation and its composition properties remain open here; no unique definition is imputed to the manuscript.''',
41:r'''Let f:X→S be proper, flat and finitely presented, and let Z→X be affine, with no finiteness assumption on Z/X. Then T↦Hom_(X_T)(X_T,Z_T), for S-schemes T, is represented by an affine S-scheme, compatibly with arbitrary base change. Here Hom means sections over X_T, equivalently O_(X_T)-algebra maps from the pulled-back affine algebra to O_(X_T).''',
42:r'''Under H035.41, if Z→X is of finite type, its restriction of scalars is of finite type over S. If Z→X is finitely presented, its restriction is finitely presented over S. No noetherianity of S is required. The proof uses finitely presented module generators and, separately, finitely generated algebra relations.''',
43:r'''Under H035.41, a closed immersion Z→Z′ of affine X-schemes induces a closed immersion between their representing S-schemes of sections. The assertion permits arbitrary quasi-coherent defining ideals and arbitrary base changes; it does not assert finite presentation of the closed immersion.''',
44:r'''For f:X→S proper, flat and finitely presented, the restriction of the linear cone V(F)=Spec_X Sym(F) is V(P_F), where P_F represents Hom(F,O_X⊗f^*M), with f_* inserted in the sheaf formula on S. F may be quasi-coherent; finite presentation of F gives finite presentation of P_F. Calling V(F) a vector bundle for coherent F is false in general: F=A/(t) over A=k[t] gives A[x]/(tx). Every affine finite-type X-scheme embeds in such a cone with finitely presented F when X is quasi-compact and quasi-separated.''',
45:r'''Let X→S be proper, flat and finitely presented, and Y⊂X a closed subscheme with arbitrary quasi-coherent ideal. The subfunctor of S consisting of T→S for which Y_T=X_T is represented by a closed subscheme of S, compatibly with all base changes. This is the proper special case of Murre exposition 294, Theorem 3, p.255, after the manuscript's noetherian reduction; our proof removes the noetherian-base restriction.''',
46:r'''For f:X→S proper, flat and finitely presented and any quasi-coherent F on X, the restriction of scalars of V(F) is a linear cone V(P) for a quasi-coherent module P on S. Locally, if F=colim_i F_i with finitely presented terms, P=colim_i P_i and V(P)=lim_i V(P_i). No finite-type conclusion is asserted for arbitrary F.''',
}
